Title: "The Complex Field"
Id: "8eb34ec3-71e1-4565-b43c-b53ca58ab01f"


Text: "The complex numbers $\mathbb{C}$ arise through the algebraic completion of the real
       continuum $\mathbb{R}$. While the real numbers resolve all topological deficiencies
       via Cauchy completeness, they remain algebraically incomplete: the quadratic equation
       $x^2 + 1 = 0$ admits no solution among real magnitudes. By formally adjoining an
       ideal element $i$—the imaginary unit satisfying $i^2 = -1$—we obtain the quotient ring
       $\mathbb{R}[t]/(t^2 + 1)$, which is a field because the quadratic polynomial $t^2 + 1$
       is irreducible over $\mathbb{R}$.

       Alternatively, following William Rowan Hamilton (1835), the complex numbers can be
       constructed purely constructively as pairs of real numbers $(x, y) \in \mathbb{R}^2$
       equipped with the componentwise addition $(x_1, y_1) + (x_2, y_2) = (x_1 + x_2, y_1 + y_2)$
       and the twisted multiplication $(x_1, y_1) \cdot (x_2, y_2) = (x_1 x_2 - y_1 y_2, \, x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1)$.
       This construction entirely demystifies 'imaginary' quantities, embedding the real numbers
       isomorphically as the horizontal axis $x \mapsto (x, 0)$ and identifying $i = (0, 1)$ so
       that $(0, 1) \cdot (0, 1) = (-1, 0) = -1$.
       
       The resulting field $\mathbb{C}$ is algebraically closed (the Fundamental Theorem of
       Algebra) and simultaneously inherits the two-dimensional Euclidean topology of
       $\mathbb{R}^2$, synthesizing algebra, geometry, and analysis into a unified whole."
Id: "984eae6c-91ed-4fb5-8837-8169f4a2c022"


SectionTitle: "The Field of Complex Numbers"
Id: "4da5affd-d43d-45a1-a71a-4cd60b320278"


[\complex.numbers]
Defines: Cb ::= (C, 0, 1, +, *, -, /, i, neg(z_))
abstractly:
specifies:
. C is \set
. 0 "in" C
. 1 "in" C
. i "in" C
. + is \binary.operation:on{C}
. * is \binary.operation:on{C}
. - is \binary.operation:on{C}
. / is \binary.operation:on{C}
. neg is \function:on{C}:to{C}
expresses:
. 0 != 1
. i * i = -1
. forAll: a, b, c "in" C
  then:
  . (a + b) + c = a + (b + c)
  . a + b = b + a
  . a + 0 = a
  . (a * b) * c = a * (b * c)
  . a * b = b * a
  . a * 1 = a
  . a * (b + c) = (a * b) + (a * c)
Enables:
. capability: z_ "in" Cb :-> z_ is \complex
. view:
  as: S := C is \set
Documented:
. called: "the complex numbers"
. written: "\mathbb{C}"
. description: "The field of complex numbers, realizable as
                $\mathbb{R}[t]/(t^2+1)$ with $i$ the residue class of $t$."
Id: "19cb593a-f9c4-4ac8-82b7-2f32e590b67a"


[\complex]
Declares: z "in" \complex.numbers
Enables:
. capability: a_ + b_ :=> a_ \.complex.+./ b_
. capability: a_ - b_ :=> a_ \.complex.-./ b_
. capability: a_ * b_ :=> a_ \.complex.*./ b_
. capability: a_ / b_ :=> a_ \.complex.div./ b_
. capability: -a_ :=> \complex.neg(a_)
Documented:
. called: "complex"
. description: "A complex number is an element of $\mathbb{C}$."
Id: "af678a67-39ff-4563-a687-c4019d154b5a"


[\complex.zero]
Defines: z := \complex.numbers..0 is \complex
Documented:
. called: "complex zero"
. written: "0"
Id: "f278c550-28e5-44ec-93d2-4842994848fb"


[\complex.one]
Defines: o := \complex.numbers..1 is \complex
Documented:
. called: "complex one"
. written: "1"
Id: "09630bb3-b7f2-4eb9-b9c7-b0ccb59b191f"


[a_ \.complex.+./ b_]
Defines: a_ + b_ := \complex.numbers..`+`(a_, b_) is \complex
Documented:
. called: "$a_?$ plus $b_?$"
. written: "a_? + b_?"
Id: "10bb451f-8177-4d04-81a7-2ac67de483d0"


[a_ \.complex.-./ b_]
Defines: a_ - b_ := \complex.numbers..`-`(a_, b_) is \complex
Documented:
. called: "$a_?$ minus $b_?$"
. written: "a_? - b_?"
Id: "6fd7f728-8217-40bf-9d81-750e40ea2b6e"


[a_ \.complex.*./ b_]
Defines: a_ * b_ := \complex.numbers..`*`(a_, b_) is \complex
Documented:
. called: "$a_?$ times $b_?$"
. written: "a_? \cdot b_?"
Id: "27f9f3f8-5272-4083-9fa0-e7a4948a4362"


[a_ \.complex.div./ b_]
Defines: a_ / b_ := \complex.numbers..`/`(a_, b_) is \complex
Documented:
. called: "$a_?$ divided by $b_?$"
. written: "\frac{a_?}{b_?}"
Id: "16fc3b48-1abc-4a63-b4d4-b4c5c144b20b"


[\complex.neg(a_)]
Defines: n := \complex.numbers..neg(a_) is \complex
Documented:
. called: "negative of $a_?$"
. written: "-a_?"
Id: "c849f3cc-4b38-40ae-8f55-aa9168f00682"


[\complex.i]
Defines: unit := \complex.numbers..i is \complex
Documented:
. called: "imaginary unit"
. written: "i"
. description: "The imaginary unit $i$ satisfying $i^2 = -1$."
Id: "b5855839-892d-4a4b-a116-7a66ef02df5d"


SectionTitle: "Real Part, Imaginary Part, and Conjugation"
Id: "e5125a7d-d915-4ca8-a6ce-6c928e275445"


[\complex.real.part(z_)]
Defines: x is \real
expresses:
. exists: y is \real
  suchThat: z_ = x \.complex.+./ (y \.complex.*./ \complex.i)
Documented:
. called: "real part of $z_?$"
. written: "\operatorname{Re}(z_?)"
. description: "The real component $\operatorname{Re}(z)$ of a complex number."
Id: "ac392c5f-3f42-42d6-8313-5e4cbd764f89"


[\complex.imaginary.part(z_)]
Defines: y is \real
expresses:
. exists: x is \real
  suchThat: z_ = x \.complex.+./ (y \.complex.*./ \complex.i)
Documented:
. called: "imaginary part of $z_?$"
. written: "\operatorname{Im}(z_?)"
. description: "The imaginary component $\operatorname{Im}(z)$ of a complex
                number."
Id: "962d90fc-e3db-40b5-8c43-91c21277ac0a"


[\complex.conjugate(z_)]
Defines: zbar is \complex
expresses: zbar = \complex.real.part(z_) \.complex.-./ (\complex.imaginary.part(z_) \.complex.*./ \complex.i)
Documented:
. called: "complex conjugate of $z_?$"
. written: "\overline{z_?}"
. description: "The complex conjugate
                $\overline{z} = \operatorname{Re}(z) - i \operatorname{Im}(z)$."
Id: "45f1544a-4db7-477a-8d89-b42698988732"


[\complex.modulus(z_)]
Defines: r is \real
expresses:
. r \:real.geq?:/ \real.zero
. (r \.real.*./ r) = (\complex.real.part(z_) \.real.*./ \complex.real.part(z_)) \.real.+./ (\complex.imaginary.part(z_) \.real.*./ \complex.imaginary.part(z_))
Documented:
. called: "modulus of $z_?$"
. written: "\left| z_? \right|"
. description: "The modulus (absolute value)
                $|z| = \sqrt{\operatorname{Re}(z)^2 + \operatorname{Im}(z)^2}$."
Id: "e5faf733-3580-4323-921a-0809252cada9"


SectionTitle: "Geometry and Polar Form"
Id: "dfffea1e-10b7-4e61-a9af-08dec4a3de2e"


Theorem:
given: z is \complex
then:
. existsUnique: x is \real
  suchThat:
  . existsUnique: y is \real
    suchThat: z = x \.complex.+./ (y \.complex.*./ \complex.i)
Documented:
. called: "Uniqueness of Cartesian Form"
. description: "Every complex number has a unique representation $x+iy$ with
                real coordinates $x$ and $y$."
Proof: """Existence follows directly from the construction of
          $\mathbb{C} = \mathbb{R} \oplus i\mathbb{R}$, where
          $x = \operatorname{Re}(z)$ and $y = \operatorname{Im}(z)$.

          To establish uniqueness, suppose that $z = x_1 + i y_1 = x_2 + i y_2$
          for real numbers $x_1, x_2, y_1, y_2 \in \mathbb{R}$. Rearranging this
          equality in the field $\mathbb{C}$ yields:
          $$ (x_1 - x_2) = i(y_2 - y_1). $$ Squaring both sides and using
          $i^2 = -1$, we obtain:
          $$ (x_1 - x_2)^2 = i^2 (y_2 - y_1)^2 = -(y_2 - y_1)^2. $$ Since
          $x_1 - x_2$ and $y_2 - y_1$ are real numbers, their squares are
          non-negative: $(x_1 - x_2)^2 \ge 0$ and $-(y_2 - y_1)^2 \le 0$. The
          only real number that is simultaneously non-negative and non-positive
          is zero. Therefore: $$ (x_1 - x_2)^2 = 0 \implies x_1 = x_2, $$ and
          $$ -(y_2 - y_1)^2 = 0 \implies y_1 = y_2. $$ Thus the real and
          imaginary parts are uniquely determined."""
Id: "cb72d0b1-7ab5-4a88-9a38-cc75cdeee392"


Text: "While the Cartesian representation $z = x + iy$ reflects the additive vector space
       structure of $\mathbb{C} \cong \mathbb{R}^2$, multiplication is fundamentally geometric
       and best understood in polar coordinates $(r, \theta)$. Every nonzero complex number
       $z \in \mathbb{C} \setminus \{0\}$ admits a polar representation:
       $$ z = r(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta) = r e^{i\theta}, $$
       where $r = |z| = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2} > 0$ is the uniquely determined modulus, and
       $\theta \in \mathbb{R}$ is the *argument*, denoted $\arg(z)$.
       
       Because the trigonometric functions sine and cosine are periodic with period $2\pi$,
       the argument is determined only modulo $2\pi\mathbb{Z}$: $\theta_1 \equiv \theta_2 \pmod{2\pi}$.
       To make the argument single-valued, one selects a branch cut—most commonly the
       *principal argument* $\operatorname{Arg}(z) \in (-\pi, \pi]$, which is discontinuous along
       the negative real axis $(-\infty, 0]$.
       
       Euler's formula, $e^{i\theta} = \cos\theta + i\sin\theta$, reveals that complex multiplication
       acts as an infinitesimal rotation-dilation:
       $$ (r_1 e^{i\theta_1}) \cdot (r_2 e^{i\theta_2}) = (r_1 r_2) e^{i(\theta_1 + \theta_2)}. $$
       Moduli multiply, while arguments add. Consequently, the unit circle
       $\mathbb{T} = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| = 1\}$ forms a multiplicative subgroup isomorphic to
       the circle group $U(1)$, and taking powers yields de Moivre's formula $(e^{i\theta})^n = e^{in\theta}$.
       For each positive integer $n$, the polynomial equation $z^n = 1$ possesses exactly $n$ distinct
       solutions—the $n$-th roots of unity $\zeta_k = e^{2\pi i k / n}$ for $k = 0, 1, \ldots, n-1$—which
       form the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed in the unit circle."
Id: "7fd16fbf-1c34-4001-8ec4-c2b603e766b7"


Theorem:
given: z, w is \complex
then:
. \complex.conjugate(z \.complex.+./ w) = \complex.conjugate(z) \.complex.+./ \complex.conjugate(w)
. \complex.conjugate(z \.complex.*./ w) = \complex.conjugate(z) \.complex.*./ \complex.conjugate(w)
. z \.complex.*./ \complex.conjugate(z) = \complex.modulus(z) \.real.*./ \complex.modulus(z)
. if: z != \complex.zero
  then: \complex.one \.complex.div./ z = \complex.conjugate(z) \.complex.div./ (\complex.modulus(z) \.real.*./ \complex.modulus(z))
Documented:
. called: "Algebraic Laws of Complex Conjugation"
. description: "Conjugation preserves addition and multiplication, and its
                product with the original number is the squared modulus."
Proof: """
  Let $z = x_1 + i y_1$ and $w = x_2 + i y_2$ with $x_1, x_2, y_1, y_2 \in \mathbb{R}$.

  1. **Additivity**: We compute:
     $$ z + w = (x_1 + x_2) + i(y_1 + y_2), $$
     so by definition of conjugation:
     $$ \overline{z + w} = (x_1 + x_2) - i(y_1 + y_2) = (x_1 - i y_1) + (x_2 - i y_2) = \overline{z} + \overline{w}. $$

  2. **Multiplicativity**: By definition of complex multiplication:
     $$ zw = (x_1 x_2 - y_1 y_2) + i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1). $$
     Its conjugate is:
     $$ \overline{zw} = (x_1 x_2 - y_1 y_2) - i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1). $$
     On the other hand, expanding $\overline{z} \cdot \overline{w}$:
     $$ (x_1 - i y_1)(x_2 - i y_2) = (x_1 x_2 + (-y_1)(-y_2)i^2) - i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1) = (x_1 x_2 - y_1 y_2) - i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1). $$
     Thus $\overline{zw} = \overline{z} \cdot \overline{w}$.

  3. **Product with Conjugate**: Computing directly:
     $$ z \overline{z} = (x_1 + i y_1)(x_1 - i y_1) = x_1^2 - (i y_1)^2 = x_1^2 + y_1^2 = |z|^2. $$

  4. **Multiplicative Inverse**: If $z \ne 0$, then $|z|^2 > 0$ is a non-zero real number.
     Dividing the identity $z \overline{z} = |z|^2$ by $z |z|^2$ yields:
     $$ z^{-1} = \frac{1}{z} = \frac{\overline{z}}{|z|^2}. $$
"""
Id: "bdb7d794-5eb2-41f5-823f-5f4f719266a3"


Text: "Geometrically, complex conjugation $z \mapsto \overline{z}$ is the
       reflection of the Argand plane across the real horizontal axis,
       preserving distances and magnitudes while reversing planar orientation
       and the sign of angles.

       Algebraically, conjugation is a non-trivial field automorphism of
       $\mathbb{C}$ that fixes every real number pointwise: $\sigma(x) = x$ for
       all $x \in \mathbb{R}$, and $\sigma(i) = -i$. Because
       $\sigma^2 = \operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{C}}$, conjugation is an
       involution, generating the two-element Galois group
       $\operatorname{Gal}(\mathbb{C}/\mathbb{R}) \cong \mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$.
       In particular, if a polynomial $P(z) \in \mathbb{R}[z]$ has real
       coefficients, then $P(\overline{z}) = \overline{P(z)}$, which guarantees
       that all non-real roots of real polynomials must occur in complex
       conjugate pairs."
Id: "b6cf05ac-3997-42c1-8a87-d6cc236431f1"


Theorem:
given: z, w is \complex
then:
. \complex.modulus(z \.complex.*./ w) = \complex.modulus(z) \.real.*./ \complex.modulus(w)
. \complex.modulus(z \.complex.+./ w) \:real.leq?:/ \complex.modulus(z) \.real.+./ \complex.modulus(w)
Documented:
. called: "Multiplicativity and Triangle Inequality for the Complex Modulus"
. description: "The complex modulus is multiplicative and subadditive."
Proof: """
  1. **Multiplicativity**: Using the algebraic properties of conjugation:
     $$ |zw|^2 = (zw)\overline{(zw)} = (zw)(\overline{z} \, \overline{w}) = (z\overline{z})(w\overline{w}) = |z|^2 |w|^2. $$
     Taking the non-negative square root of both sides gives $|zw| = |z||w|$.

  2. **Triangle Inequality (Subadditivity)**: For any complex number $u$, we have
     $\operatorname{Re}(u) \le \sqrt{\operatorname{Re}(u)^2 + \operatorname{Im}(u)^2} = |u|$.
     Expanding the squared modulus of $z + w$:
     $$ |z + w|^2 = (z + w)\overline{(z + w)} = (z + w)(\overline{z} + \overline{w}) = z\overline{z} + z\overline{w} + w\overline{z} + w\overline{w}. $$
     Note that $w\overline{z} = \overline{z\overline{w}}$, so $z\overline{w} + w\overline{z} = 2\operatorname{Re}(z\overline{w})$.
     Therefore:
     $$ |z + w|^2 = |z|^2 + 2\operatorname{Re}(z\overline{w}) + |w|^2. $$
     Applying the bound $\operatorname{Re}(z\overline{w}) \le |z\overline{w}| = |z||\overline{w}| = |z||w|$:
     $$ |z + w|^2 \le |z|^2 + 2|z||w| + |w|^2 = (|z| + |w|)^2. $$
     Taking the non-negative square root of both sides yields the triangle inequality:
     $$ |z + w| \le |z| + |w|. $$
"""
Id: "d366bd62-6a88-4090-9613-ac5843473195"


Text: "Equipped with the distance function $d(z, w) = |z - w|$, the complex plane $(\mathbb{C}, d)$
       becomes a complete metric space isometric to the two-dimensional Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^2$.
       The metric topology is generated by open disks $D(z_0, r) = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z - z_0| < r\}$.
       
       For global analysis and the study of rational and meromorphic functions, it is essential to
       compactify $\mathbb{C}$ by adjoining a single ideal point at infinity, $\infty$. The resulting
       *extended complex plane* $\widehat{\mathbb{C}} = \mathbb{C} \cup \{\infty\}$ is homeomorphic
       to the two-dimensional unit sphere $S^2 = \{(X, Y, Z) \in \mathbb{R}^3 : X^2 + Y^2 + Z^2 = 1\}$
       via *stereographic projection* from the north pole $(0, 0, 1)$:
       $$ z = \frac{X + iY}{1 - Z}, \quad (X, Y, Z) = \left(\frac{2\operatorname{Re}(z)}{|z|^2 + 1}, \, \frac{2\operatorname{Im}(z)}{|z|^2 + 1}, \, \frac{|z|^2 - 1}{|z|^2 + 1}\right). $$
       Under this projection—known as the *Riemann sphere*—circles on the sphere correspond bijectively
       to circles and straight lines in the plane, and the group of conformal automorphisms is precisely
       the group of Möbius transformations $z \mapsto \frac{az + b}{cz + d}$ with $ad - bc \ne 0$."
Id: "bef527e9-6799-4ccb-95e3-708a6e326e89"


TextTheorem: "Fundamental Theorem of Algebra. Let $P(z) = a_n z^n + a_{n-1} z^{n-1} + \cdots + a_1 z + a_0$
              be a nonconstant polynomial with complex coefficients ($a_n \ne 0$ and $n \ge 1$).
              Then $P(z)$ has at least one root in $\mathbb{C}$, that is, there exists $z_0 \in \mathbb{C}$
              such that $P(z_0) = 0$.
              
              Consequently, by polynomial long division and induction on the degree, every degree-$n$
              polynomial factors completely into $n$ linear factors:
              $$ P(z) = a_n (z - \alpha_1)(z - \alpha_2) \cdots (z - \alpha_n), $$
              where $\alpha_1, \ldots, \alpha_n \in \mathbb{C}$ are the roots counted with multiplicity.
              In the language of field theory, this theorem establishes that $\mathbb{C}$ is *algebraically closed*,
              forming the algebraic closure of $\mathbb{R}$ with $[\mathbb{C} : \mathbb{R}] = 2$.
              
              *Historical and Conceptual Proof Idea*: First claimed by Peter Roth (1608) and Albert Girard (1629),
              and partially proved by Jean le Rond d'Alembert (1746), the first rigorous proof was completed
              by Carl Friedrich Gauss in 1799. Despite its name, every known proof requires topological or
              analytic input: the continuous growth of $|P(z)| \to \infty$ as $|z| \to \infty$ forces $|P(z)|$
              to attain its global minimum at some point $z_0 \in \mathbb{C}$. If $P(z_0) = c \ne 0$, a local
              Taylor expansion $P(z_0 + h) = c + b h^k + O(h^{k+1})$ allows choosing $h$ so that $b h^k$ has
              direction opposite to $c$, producing a value $|P(z_0 + h)| < |c|$, which contradicts the
              minimality of $|P(z_0)|$ (d'Alembert's Lemma). In subsequent sections, we shall witness how
              Liouville's Theorem and Cauchy's Residue Theorem yield effortless one-line proofs of this milestone."
Id: "78bb4229-6dba-4214-be87-a716e457565a"
